cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Thinker: advising what to run next from what a run did"
message: "If you use the thinker in your research, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Gursoy
    given-names: Doga
    affiliation: Argonne National Laboratory
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0080-8101"
repository-code: "https://github.com/open-cora/thinker"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-26"
keywords:
  - beamline-automation
  - synchrotron
  - hexagonal-architecture
  - ports-and-adapters
  - autonomous-experimentation
abstract: >-
  The thinker reads one finished execution from a system of record, pairs
  what each step was asked to do with what became of it, and concludes what
  should happen next. A conclusion is one of four: propose a run, stop
  because the goal is met, abstain because nothing is warranted, or refer
  the case to a person. Only the first is something the record can hold
  today, and the other three are carried in the type so that the vocabulary
  is settled before the storage is. Whatever forms the conclusion sits
  behind a seam, so which inference provider a deployment runs is a choice
  made at its entrypoint rather than a fact the domain knows. It is a client
  of the keeper, a separate deployable bound to it by prose rather than by a
  shared package.
